# peripheral(3)

## NAME

peripheral - use devices from Lua

## SYNOPSIS

    peripheral.getNames()
    peripheral.isPresent(name)
    peripheral.getType(name)
    peripheral.hasType(name, type)
    peripheral.getMethods(name)
    peripheral.call(name, method, ...)
    peripheral.wrap(name)
    peripheral.find(type [, filter])
    peripheral.getName(wrapped)
    peripheral.bytes(s)

## DESCRIPTION

The `peripheral` library of lua(1) lists and calls the devices connected to the computer. It follows the `peripheral` API of CC: Tweaked. It is a global and is also returned by `require("peripheral")`.

A device is named by its address, a lower-case UUID such as `0a1b2c3d-...`. Upper-case letters are accepted and turned into lower case. The type of a device is its kind, such as `kibicomputers:screen`.

- `getNames()`: a table of the addresses of the connected devices
- `isPresent(name)`: whether a device is connected at the address
- `getType(name)`: the kind of the device, or nil
- `hasType(name, type)`: whether the kind is `type`. `type` matches the whole kind or the part after `:` (`screen` matches `kibicomputers:screen`). nil if no device is connected
- `getMethods(name)`: a table of the method names, or nil
- `call(name, method, ...)`: calls the method with the arguments and returns its results
- `wrap(name)`: a table with one function per method, or nil. Call them with a dot: `p.getSize()`
- `find(type [, filter])`: wraps every device whose kind matches `type` (as in `hasType`) and returns them as multiple values. With `filter`, only the devices for which `filter(name, wrapped)` returns true
- `getName(wrapped)`: the address of a wrapped device. `getType`, `hasType` and `getMethods` also accept a wrapped device
- `bytes(s)`: wraps the string `s` so that it is passed as bytes instead of text

Method names are in kebab case (`get-size`). `wrap` also adds a camel case alias (`getSize`), and `call` accepts both forms.

A call to a method that runs on the main thread of the server waits at least one game tick. Waiting does not use a thread.

## VALUES

- nil: none
- boolean: boolean
- integer: integer
- float: float. A float with an integer value (`10/2`) is sent as an integer
- string: text. It must be valid UTF-8
- `peripheral.bytes(s)`: bytes

Results come back the same way; text and bytes both become strings. Tables and functions cannot be passed. A call takes at most 32 arguments and 64 KiB of strings, and a method name is at most 64 bytes.

## ERRORS

A failed call raises a Lua error:

- `No such peripheral`: no device is connected at the address, or a different kind of device was connected there after `wrap`
- `No such method NAME`: the device has no such method
- the message of the device: the arguments were wrong, or the method failed
- `interrupted`: the world was saved and loaded while the method was running
- `not enough memory`: the result might not fit in the memory of the computer
- `device call failed: ERROR`: another error of the system

## DIFFERENCES FROM CC: TWEAKED

- Names are addresses, not sides (`left`) or network names
- A device has one type, its kind
- Strings that are not UTF-8 must be wrapped with `peripheral.bytes`
- Tables cannot be passed to or returned from a device

## SEE ALSO

